Gradle — the script-based alternative.

Maven describes a build in declarative X-M-L. Gradle takes the opposite bet: a build is a program, written in a real scripting language — so anything you can express in code, you can express in your build. It shares Maven's whole dependency model, but trades rigid convention for programmable flexibility, and considerably more speed. This section is Gradle, as the counterpoint to Maven.

A Gradle build lives in a build-dot-gradle file, written in Groovy, or a build-dot-gradle-dot-k-t-s file, written in the Kotlin domain-specific language. And it reads like configuration, but it is executable code. The dependency block looks familiar, because the model underneath is the same — you list, for instance, Guava as an implementation dependency, and J-unit as a test dependency. That's the identical group, artifact, version coordinate from Maven, just on one line. And because the script is code, you can wrap it in loops, conditionals, and custom logic when a build genuinely needs them.

Here's the crucial point: Gradle did not reinvent the dependency world. It resolves the same coordinates, from the same repositories — Maven Central and friends — with the same transitive resolution. So switching build tools doesn't switch libraries; it only switches how you declare and drive the build. Gradle also follows the same conventional source-main-java layout by default. What changes is the surface — concise script instead of verbose X-M-L, and a programming model instead of a fixed lifecycle.

So why do teams pick it? Two headline advantages: performance, and flexibility. On performance: Gradle does incremental builds — it tracks inputs and outputs, and re-runs only the tasks whose inputs actually changed. It has a build cache, so it can reuse task outputs across builds, and even across machines — work done once, by you or a teammate, isn't repeated. And it runs a daemon, a background process that stays warm between builds, avoiding java-virtual-machine startup each time. On flexibility: custom build steps are just code, so unconventional builds don't fight the tool. Together, these make Gradle noticeably faster on large projects, and far more comfortable when a build strays off the beaten path.

But nothing is free. That same flexibility means a Gradle build can grow into complex, hard-to-follow script, and there's simply more to learn than Maven's fixed model — a build-as-program can go wrong in ways a declarative P-O-M can't. So the honest summary is a spectrum, not a winner. Maven favors convention and predictability: every project looks the same, which is a virtue for straightforward apps, and for teams that value uniformity. Gradle favors flexibility and speed: it's the standard for Android, common on large or unconventional builds, and preferred where build performance matters. Both pull the same dependencies from the same repositories; the choice is really declarative X-M-L versus programmable script.

With your source compiled and packaged by a build tool, one job remains: proving the code actually works. Build tools run tests, but they don't write them. The last two sections are the frameworks that do — J-unit, for the tests themselves, and Mockito, for testing a unit in isolation.
